@@ -108,13 +108,37 @@ const result = await pRetry(run, {
 });
 ```
 
+##### onRetry(context)
+
+Type: `Function`
+
+Callback invoked only when another attempt is going to happen, right before the wait. Receives the same context object as `onFailedAttempt`. Unlike `onFailedAttempt`, it is not called for the final failure, which makes it suited to counting retries.
+
+The function is called _after_ `onFailedAttempt`, `shouldConsumeRetry`, and `shouldRetry`, and is _not_ called when the error is rethrown instead of retried. It is still called when `shouldConsumeRetry` returns `false`, since the next attempt follows straight away.
+
+The function can return a promise, which is awaited before the wait starts.
+
+If the function throws, all retries will be aborted and the original promise will reject with the thrown error.
+
+```js
+import pRetry from 'p-retry';
+
+const run = async () => { … };
+
+const result = await pRetry(run, {
+	onRetry: ({error, attemptNumber, retryDelay}) => {
+		metrics.increment('retries');
+	}
+});
+```
+
 ##### shouldRetry(context)
 
 Type: `Function`
 
 Decide if a retry should occur based on context. Returning `true` triggers a retry, `false` aborts with the error. Returning nothing counts as `false`.
 
-The function is called _after_ `onFailedAttempt` and `shouldConsumeRetry`.
+The function is called _after_ `onFailedAttempt` and `shouldConsumeRetry`, and _before_ `onRetry`.
 
 The function is _not_ called on `AbortError`, on an abort through `signal`, on `TypeError` (except network errors), or if `retries` or `maxRetryTime` are exhausted.
 
@@ -224,6 +248,17 @@ Measured with a monotonic clock (`performance.now()`) so system clock adjustment
 
 The limit is checked after failed attempts and callbacks, before scheduling a retry. The retry delay is shortened to the remaining time, but the scheduled retry can still start after the limit. Attempts and callbacks are never interrupted, so they can run past the limit.
 
+##### initialDelay
+
+Type: `number`\
+Default: `0`
+
+The number of milliseconds to wait before the first attempt. With the default of `0`, no timer is scheduled and the first attempt starts straight away.
+
+The wait is not part of the retrying, so it does not count toward `maxRetryTime`. A fractional value rounds up to a whole millisecond, and a value above 2147483647ms is clamped to that, like the retry delays.
+
+If `signal` aborts during the wait, the promise rejects with the abort reason and `input` is never called. `unref` applies to this wait too.
+
 ##### signal
 
 Type: [`AbortSignal`](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/AbortSignal)
